+## T-2379 — credited later-state picture, 2026-11-03
+
+The ticket's picture, `chicago/reference/images/chicago/chicago_fort_dearborn_river_mouth_1830.jpg`, is the 1856 Kellogg/Blanchard **Old Fort Dearborn** lithograph, not an 1830 view. Its labelled **LAKE HOUSE** is the completed hotel to the right of the foreground fort blockhouse, partly behind a tree; regular window rows and its central cupola are visible. The existing image catalog dates the depicted scene approximately 1836–1849. The NYPL-attributed Commons full scan was read directly, including its Hartford lithographer and 52 La Salle Street publisher imprints. The direct NYPL catalog request returned an access placeholder; metadata and public-domain flags were read from the credited Commons item, not represented as a fresh live NYPL catalog reading.
+
+`kellogg_blanchard_1856_old_fort_lake_house` records that identity, public-domain publication provenance and the temporal limit. Naming it in the structure's research note provides a credited Images link **for later completed-state context**. The picture is tier-5 cross-check material, not a metric survey or geometry-tracing authority. It supplies no July 1835 construction stage. The June 10, 1835 Chicago Democrat notice establishes an active named project; it does not date its height or roof. T-3123 already made roof absence reconstructed. The one-storey, four-metre, roofless shell, its dimensions and placement are unchanged.
+
+This increment does not solve a canonical camera, accept a side-by-side, promote the grade or close T-2379. A legitimate later-state comparison must keep the completed appearance out of July 1835 and respect the existing source-use limits. Original image-gate acceptance remains open.
